class:: PitchPESTO
summary:: Native PESTO neural pitch estimation with explicit model loading
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Pitch
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/Pitch, Classes/Pitch2, Classes/PitchYIN

description::
An optional MIR2 Neural analyzer for monophonic audio, adapted from Alain Riou and colleagues' PESTO v2. A 251-bin variable-Q transform feeds a residual convolutional network. The network produces a 384-bin pitch distribution; a fixed learned offset and argmax-local weighted average decode its fundamental frequency. A separate learned classifier estimates voicing confidence from spectral energy and flatness. The selected checkpoint is code::mir-1k_g7::, trained on MIR-1K.

The runtime is native CPU code. It uses neither Python nor an external inference framework, and never downloads a model. The offline exporter in code::MIR2/neural/tools/export_pesto.py:: produces a sample-rate-specific float32 WAV containing weights and frontend kernels. Load that data explicitly before starting the Synth. The installed extension's README explains the export procedure; the example below expects a local model in its code::models:: directory.

Updates occur every 10 ms. A trailing analysis window is frozen, and its inference work is distributed over the following 10 ms hop. Windows contain 4096 samples at 16 kHz, 8192 at 44.1 or 48 kHz, and 16384 at 96 kHz. Nominal window-center lag, including the extra hop, is approximately 138 ms at 16 kHz, 102.88 ms at 44.1 kHz, and 95.33 ms at 48 or 96 kHz. Control-block scheduling adds up to one block. Startup waits for a full history and its inference: about 270, 200, 190 and 190 ms respectively. Outputs are held between updates and are zero during startup. This trailing mode does not use the paper's optional refill method or claim its sub-10-ms latency.

This is an experimental monophonic estimator, not a polyphonic transcription system. Confidence is a learned score, not a calibrated probability of correct pitch. Agreement with the original model does not establish accuracy on arbitrary recordings. Low-confidence frequency is zero instead of retaining an earlier note. Preparing the model and creating the Synth incur initialization work; prepare them before a timing-critical passage.

Prefer a server block size of 128 samples at 96 kHz. The measured 64-sample run at that rate contained a deadline overrun; CPU load and other Synths affect the available margin. Native runtime portability and longer live-server endurance remain qualification work.

Like link::Classes/Pitch::, this UGen supplies a fundamental estimate and a voiced decision for synthesis control. It instead uses a learned spectral model, exposes continuous confidence and spectral energy, and has a longer explicitly defined analysis delay.

classmethods::

method:: kr

argument:: in
Mono audio-rate input. Use separate instances to analyze separate channels; each instance adds computation and state.

argument:: model
A global Buffer, or its number, containing the unmodified float32 code::model.wav:: produced by the pinned exporter. Its sample rate must match the server: 16000, 44100, 48000 or 96000 Hz. Load it and call code::s.sync:: before creating the Synth. Keep the Buffer allocated and unchanged until all Synths using it have stopped. A model is fixed for the lifetime of a Synth; several instances may share the same read-only Buffer. Do not play, normalize or resample this data.

argument:: threshold
Voicing-confidence threshold, clipped to 0 through 1; default 0.8. Higher values suppress more uncertain frames. Silence and invalid analysis also produce an unvoiced result.

returns::
An Array of four control-rate values: frequency in Hz (zero when unvoiced), learned confidence from 0 through 1, voiced flag 0 or 1, and the sum of the 251 VQT energy bins. The last value depends on input level and frontend normalization; it is neither waveform RMS nor calibrated loudness.

examples::
The original voice is on the left; a two-partial synthesized voice following the estimated pitch is on the right. Listen for pitch errors and delay as well as successful tracking. Export a model for the server's actual sample rate first, using the extension README.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot {
    Routine({
        var rate = s.sampleRate.asInteger;
        var modelPath = PitchPESTO.filenameSymbol.asString.dirname.dirname
            +/+ "models" +/+ ("mir-1k_g7-" ++ rate ++ ".wav");
        if(File.exists(modelPath).not) {
            Error("PitchPESTO: export a model for this server rate and copy it to " ++ modelPath).throw;
        };
        ~mir2PESTOModel = Buffer.read(s, modelPath);
        ~mir2Buffer = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.child);
        s.sync;
        ~mir2PESTOExample = {
            var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
            // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);
            var result = PitchPESTO.kr(source, ~mir2PESTOModel, 0.8);
            var frequency = Gate.kr(result[0], result[2]).clip(40, 2000).lag(0.03);
            var envelope = Amplitude.kr(source, 0.01, 0.12).clip(0, 0.3);
            var voice = SinOsc.ar(frequency) + (SinOsc.ar(frequency * 2) * 0.3);
            voice = voice * envelope * result[2].lag(0.02) * 0.4;
            [source * 0.2, voice] * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.4)
        }.play;
    }).play(AppClock);
};
)
::
Stop the Synth before releasing its model:
code::
(
Routine({
    ~mir2PESTOExample.free;
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Buffer.free;
    ~mir2PESTOModel.free;
}).play(AppClock);
)
::

